        <p>Offer A wins by $5,600.00 of gross and $3,921.20 of take-home, even though its rate is $2 lower. The gap is the four unpaid weeks: each one costs $2,440.00 of gross at $61, and the take-home cost of $1,667.74 a week adds up fast. Even if the $61 worker takes only three unpaid weeks, the year is $119,560.00 gross and $92,940.46 of take-home, still $2,204.66 short of the $59 job with its time off paid. The lesson is the same one this whole guide runs on: compare offers on paid weeks and real hours, not on the headline rate. A rate is only worth what its paid weeks pay.</p>
