        <p>Follow the last column from the top down and the shape of the year shows itself. At 20 hours a week you keep $49.50 an hour. At 40 hours you keep $45.74. The gap is $3.76 an hour on the same $59.00 rate, and the bracket is behind it. None of a 20-hour year’s $45,260.00 of taxable income reaches the 22% bracket. A 25-hour year carries $60,600.00, and $10,200.00 of it lands in the 22% bracket, so the 25-hour row is the first schedule on this page where the 22% bracket turns up at all. Three rows deserve a second look. A 35-hour week clears $100,000 gross on its own, at $107,380.00, and its $84,371.83 of take-home already beats a $100,000 salary by $5,191.83, before overtime or a raise. A 37.5-hour paid week, the 7.5-hour office day, pays $115,050.00 gross, still ahead of a $110,000 salary by $5,050.00 gross and $3,552.67 of take-home, and giving back 2.5 hours a week costs $7,670.00 gross and $5,377.45 of take-home. The move from 30 to 40 hours is worth $30,680.00 gross and $21,564.98 of take-home, or $1,797.08 a month, the single biggest lever on this page, bigger than any raise in the nearby-rates table. Part-time keeps the bigger share: at 20 hours you keep 83.9% of gross, against 77.5% full-time, and a 25-hour week at $59 beats a $70,000 salary’s take-home by $4,713.45, while a 20-hour week beats a full-time $27-an-hour job by $4,178.20. Our <a href="/articles/how-to-convert-part-time-hourly-pay-to-annual-salary/">part-time conversion guide</a> works the same ladder for any rate and any hours, and our <a href="/articles/how-many-hours-is-full-time/">full-time-hours guide</a> explains why 30 hours is the line where benefits usually start.</p>
